{"seconds": 46.2, "exit": 0, "final": "After Qxa1, White plays Rxa1 and is up material significantly (rook + some pawns). Let me now verify the main line:\n\n**Rb4** is the winning move. After:\n1. Rb4 (attacking the queen with the rook, and the queen is also attacked by Qa1)\n   - If 1...Qxb4, then 2. axb4 wins the queen\n   - If 1...Nxh2, then 2. Rxd4 wins the queen (defended only by the rook on d8, which can't capture because Qa1 protects d4)\n   - If 1...Qxa1, then 2. Rxa1 wins the queen\n\nThis is a classic **fork** - the rook attacks the queen and opens/supports the queen's attack on the same piece. Let me write the coaching note.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Rb4\", \"line\": [\"Rb4\", \"Nxh2\", \"Rxd4\", \"Rxd4\", \"Qxa1\", \"Rxa1\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching Note:**\nWhen your rook moved to b4, it attacked the Black queen on d4, and simultaneously unblocked your queen's diagonal from a1. The queen on d4 was now attacked twice but defended only once by the rook on d8. This is a \"fork\"\u2014two pieces attacking one target defended once. Black's queen had nowhere to escape: any capture (Qxb4, Qxa1, Nxh2) loses material. Always look for moves that multiply your attackers on one piece while reducing the defenders.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "2kr3r/ppp2ppp/8/1P6/3q2n1/P2Pp3/1RP1B2P/Q4RK1 w - - 2 21"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "2kr3r/ppp2ppp/8/1P6/3q2n1/P2Pp3/1RP1B2P/Q4RK1 w - - 2 21"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2kr3r/ppp2ppp/8/1P6/3q2n1/P2Pp3/1RP1B2P/Q4RK1 w - - 2 21", "square": "f1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2kr3r/ppp2ppp/8/1P6/3q2n1/P2Pp3/1RP1B2P/Q4RK1 w - - 2 21", "square": "a1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2kr3r/ppp2ppp/8/1P6/3q2n1/P2Pp3/1RP1B2P/Q4RK1 w - - 2 21", "square": "e2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2kr3r/ppp2ppp/8/1P6/3q2n1/P2Pp